      "source": "data/inference.jsonl",
      "input": {
        "question_id": "13181_8353800587820",
        "question": "Based on the initial chest X-ray (Figure 1), which of the following findings is most indicative of an overlooked tracheal stenosis that could have been mistaken for COPD, and what reasoning supports this conclusion?\n\nA. Flattened hemi-diaphragms are the primary indicator, suggesting emphysema rather than tracheal stenosis.\nB. Hyperinflation of the chest is the main finding, which can mask tracheal stenosis under the sternal shadow.\nC. Presence of stridor on examination directly correlates with the visible tracheal stenosis on the X-ray.\nD. A well-defined mass near the clavicle signifies tracheal stenosis, unrelated to COPD presentation.\nE. Decreased air entry throughout the lung fields is the sole indicator of tracheal stenosis.\nF. The presence of bilateral pleural effusion, obscuring tracheal details, is mistaken for COPD.",
        "images": [
          "cxr_042.png"
        ]
      }
    },
